l,{"style":{"fontSize":"0.72rem","color":"#6a8fa8","fontFamily":"'DM Sans', sans-serif"},"children":["Jun 2, 2026"," · ","3 min read"]}]]}],["$","h1",null,{"style":{"fontFamily":"'Syne', sans-serif","fontWeight":900,"fontSize":"clamp(1.8rem, 6vw, 2.5rem)","lineHeight":1.15,"letterSpacing":"-0.025em","color":"#e8f4ff","marginBottom":"1rem"},"children":"Building Zero-Trust Networks from Scratch"}],["$","p",null,{"style":{"fontSize":"1rem","color":"#3d5a6e","lineHeight":1.7,"fontFamily":"'DM Sans', sans-serif","fontWeight":300},"children":"How modern enterprises eliminate implicit trust and verify every request — no matter the source."}],["$","div",null,{"style":{"height":"1px","background":"linear-gradient(90deg, #00f2fe40, transparent)","margin":"2rem 0"}}]]}],["$","article",null,{"className":"prose","style":{"paddingBottom":"5rem"},"children":[["$","p","0",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"Zero trust is not a single product you can buy. It is an architectural shift based on a simple premise: never trust, always verify."}}],["$","div","1",{"style":{"height":"0.5rem"}}],["$","h2","2",{"children":"The Death of the VPN"}],["$","div","3",{"style":{"height":"0.5rem"}}],["$","p","4",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"Traditional networks used a \"castle and moat\" approach. You use a VPN to cross the moat, and once inside the castle, you have the keys to the kingdom. "}}],["$","div","5",{"style":{"height":"0.5rem"}}],["$","p","6",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"If an attacker compromises one employee's laptop, they can pivot laterally across the entire internal network. Zero trust eliminates the moat. Every internal service is treated as if it sits on the public internet."}}],["$","div","7",{"style":{"height":"0.5rem"}}],["$","h2","8",{"children":"The Three Pillars of Zero Trust"}],["$","div","9",{"style":{"height":"0.5rem"}}],["$","p","10",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"<strong>1. Identity as the Perimeter</strong>"}}],["$","p","11",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"IP addresses mean nothing. Access is granted based on verified user identity. This requires mandatory Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) and Single Sign-On (SSO)."}}],["$","div","12",{"style":{"height":"0.5rem"}}],["$","p","13",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"<strong>2. Device Posture</strong>"}}],["$","p","14",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"Even if the user is verified, is their device safe? A zero-trust proxy checks if the laptop has the latest OS updates and active antivirus before granting access."}}],["$","div","15",{"style":{"height":"0.5rem"}}],["$","p","16",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"<strong>3. Micro-Segmentation</strong>"}}],["$","p","17",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"Networks are chopped into tiny, isolated segments. A compromised web server cannot talk to the database server unless a strict policy explicitly allows it."}}],["$","div","18",{"style":{"height":"0.5rem"}}],["$","h2","19",{"children":"Practical Implementation: Access Policies"}],["$","div","20",{"style":{"height":"0.5rem"}}],["$","p","21",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"Instead of broad firewall rules, zero-trust uses dynamic access policies. Here is an example of what a zero-trust policy structure looks like in JSON:"}}],["$","div","22",{"style":{"height":"0.5rem"}}],["$","p","23",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"<code></code>`json"}}],["$","p","24",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"{"}}],["$","p","25",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"  \"policy_name\": \"Restrict_DB_Access\","}}],["$","p","26",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"  \"action\": \"allow\","}}],["$","p","27",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"  \"principals\": [\"role:database_admin\"],"}}],["$","p","28",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"  \"conditions\": {"}}],["$","p","29",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"    \"device_compliant\": true,"}}],["$","p","30",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"    \"location\": [\"KE\", \"ZA\"],"}}],["$","p","31",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"    \"mfa_recently_verified\": true"}}],["$","p","32",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"  },"}}],["$","p","33",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"  \"resource\": \"arn:internal:db:customer_data\""}}],["$","p","34",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"}"}}],["$","p","35",{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"<code></code>`"}}],["$","div","36",{"style":{"height":"0.5rem"}}],["$","h2","37",{"children":"Getting Started in Your Environment"}],["$","div","38",{"style":{"height":"0.5rem"}}],["$","ol","39",{"children":["$","li",null,{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"<strong>Map the flows:</strong> Understand exactly which applications need to talk to each other."}}]}],["$","ol","40",{"children":["$","li",null,{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"<strong>Implement an Identity Provider:</strong> Centralize authentication (e.g., Okta, Entra ID)."}}]}],["$","ol","41",{"children":["$","li",null,{"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"<strong>Deploy an Identity-Aware Proxy:</strong> Put a gateway in front of internal apps so users authenticate before the network connection is even made."}}]}]]}],["$","$L9",null,{}]]}]]}]]
